  {
   "id": "do-now",
   "label": "Do Now: Character Sets",
   "type": "short-answer-validation",
   "validatorId": "y10-13l1-do-now",
   "validatorKey": "y10_1_3_l1_do_now",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Do Now: Character Sets</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Recapping 1.2 Character Sets. Cambridge IGCSE 0478/13, June 2026, Question 1(c)(i): a computer uses ASCII for a text file. One word from the file is &quot;cat&quot;. The ASCII denary value for the character &quot;c&quot; is 99.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l1-do-now-a\">(a) Give the ASCII denary value for &quot;a&quot;. [1]</label><input id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-a\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Character Sets part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l1-do-now-b\">(b) Give the ASCII denary value for &quot;t&quot;. [1]</label><input id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-b\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Character Sets part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 2 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"
  },
  {
   "id": "do-now-2",
   "label": "Do Now: Images and Text",
   "type": "multiple-choice",
   "containerId": "y10-13l1-do-now-2-mcq",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Do Now: Images and Text</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Recapping 1.2 Pixel Lab and Character Sets. Based on Cambridge IGCSE 0478/12, June 2025, Question 2(c)(i) and 0478/13, June 2026, Question 1(c)(ii).</p><div id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-2-mcq\"></div>",
   "items": [
    {
     "prompt": "An image file has a colour depth of 32 bits. What does this mean?",
     "options": [
      "The image is 32 pixels wide",
      "32 bits are used to store the colour of each pixel",
      "The image can only use 32 colours",
      "The whole image is stored in 32 bits"
     ],
     "correct": 1,
     "explain": "Colour depth is the number of bits used for each pixel's colour. 32 bits per pixel gives 2 to the power 32 possible colours, not 32."
    },
    {
     "prompt": "A computer uses ASCII for a text file. Identify one other character set it could use.",
     "options": [
      "Hexadecimal",
      "Binary",
      "Denary",
      "Unicode"
     ],
     "correct": 3,
     "explain": "Unicode is the other character set on the syllabus. It covers far more characters, such as other languages and emojis, but needs more bits per character."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "do-now-3",
   "label": "Do Now: Hexadecimal",
   "type": "short-answer-validation",
   "validatorId": "y10-13l1-do-now-3",
   "validatorKey": "y10_1_3_l1_do_now_3",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Do Now: Hexadecimal</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">A question from 1.1 Number Systems. Cambridge IGCSE 0478/11, June 2025, Question 2(b)(i): two ticket numbers are 14B and 97A. Convert the two ticket numbers from hexadecimal to binary.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l1-do-now-3-a\">(a) 14B [1]</label><input id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-3-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-3-a\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Hexadecimal part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l1-do-now-3-b\">(b) 97A [1]</label><input id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-3-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-3-b\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Hexadecimal part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-3-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 2 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10-13l1-do-now-3-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"
  },
  {
   "id": "title",
   "label": "Storage Units",
   "content": "<div class=\"lesson-title-slide\"><p class=\"lesson-title-kicker\">Data Storage and Compression</p><h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Storage Units</h2><p>Year 10, 1.3 L1 &middot; Data Storage &middot; 30 minutes</p></div><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li><strong>Concept:</strong> Every unit from a bit up to an exbibyte, and how many of each make the next.</li><li><strong>Walkthrough:</strong> Convert across several units in one go.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Storage Blitz, the units level.</li><li><strong>Exam link:</strong> Three real Cambridge questions on storage units.</li></ul>"
  },
  {
   "id": "concept",
   "label": "The Storage Unit Ladder",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">The Storage Unit Ladder</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Data storage is measured in binary units. Each unit is made from a fixed number of the one below it:</p><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li><strong>1 nibble</strong> = 4 bits &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <strong>1 byte</strong> = 8 bits = 2 nibbles</li><li><strong>1 kibibyte (KiB)</strong> = 1024 bytes &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <strong>1 mebibyte (MiB)</strong> = 1024 KiB</li><li><strong>1 gibibyte (GiB)</strong> = 1024 MiB &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <strong>1 tebibyte (TiB)</strong> = 1024 GiB</li><li><strong>1 pebibyte (PiB)</strong> = 1024 TiB &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <strong>1 exbibyte (EiB)</strong> = 1024 PiB</li></ul><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Going <strong>down</strong> to a smaller unit, multiply. Going <strong>up</strong> to a bigger unit, divide. From the byte upwards, every step is 1024.</p>"
  },
  {
   "id": "exam-units",
   "label": "Exam Question: Smallest Unit",
   "type": "short-answer-validation",
   "validatorId": "y10-13l1-exam-units",
   "validatorKey": "y10_1_3_l1_exam_units",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam Question: Smallest Unit</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge IGCSE 0478/11, June 2025, Question 1(a) and (b). The size of a file can be measured using different data storage units.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l1-exam-units-a\">(a) Which is the smallest data storage unit? A byte, B gibibyte (GiB), C kibibyte (KiB), D nibble. Enter the letter. [1]</label><input id=\"y10-13l1-exam-units-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"exam-units-a\" aria-label=\"Exam Question: Smallest Unit part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l1-exam-units-b\">(b) State how many tebibytes (TiB) are equal to 2 pebibytes (PiB). [1]</label><input id=\"y10-13l1-exam-units-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"exam-units-b\" aria-label=\"Exam Question: Smallest Unit part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10-13l1-exam-units-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 2 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10-13l1-exam-units-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"
  },
  {
   "id": "concept-1024",
   "label": "Always 1024, Never 1000",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Always 1024, Never 1000</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Computers count in powers of 2, so each step up the ladder is 2<sup>10</sup> = 1024, not 1000.</p><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li>Cambridge mark schemes require 1024 in every storage calculation.</li><li>Dividing by 1000 instead gives a wrong answer, even when the method is right.</li><li>Always give the answer in the unit the question asks for.</li></ul>"
  },
  {
   "id": "check-1024",
   "label": "Check: Spot the Mistake",
   "type": "multiple-choice",
   "containerId": "y10-13l1-check-1024-mcq",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Check: Spot the Mistake</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Two students made a mistake. Choose what went wrong.</p><div id=\"y10-13l1-check-1024-mcq\"></div>",
   "items": [
    {
     "prompt": "A student converts 2048 bytes to KiB and writes 2.048 KiB. What went wrong?",
     "options": [
      "They should have multiplied by 1024",
      "They divided by 1000 instead of 1024",
      "They should have divided by 8 first",
      "Nothing, 2.048 KiB is correct"
     ],
     "correct": 1,
     "explain": "2048 / 1024 = 2 KiB. Dividing by 1000 is the decimal kilobyte, which the exam does not accept."
    },
    {
     "prompt": "A student converts 3 MiB to KiB and writes 0.003 MiB. What went wrong?",
     "options": [
      "They divided when they should have multiplied by 1024",
      "They should have multiplied by 8",
      "They used the wrong number of nibbles",
      "Nothing, the answer is correct"
     ],
     "correct": 0,
     "explain": "Going down to a smaller unit means multiplying: 3 x 1024 = 3072 KiB."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
